WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.160
- Hello, my name is Jason Schwartzman.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.540
- I'm Adam Stockhausen.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.726
- And this is "Trade Secrets."

00:00:05.726 --> 00:00:08.893
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.030
You create the entire environment.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.000
It was totally built.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.920
It's a rare thing to be, like,

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.764
in a set that was completely built

00:00:18.764 --> 00:00:23.370
with functioning water,
electricity, bathroom,

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.710
built in a place where
that didn't exist before.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:26.556
- Right.

00:00:26.556 --> 00:00:30.450
- Can I just ask very
quickly, what was it before?

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:32.970
Did you have to go and
scope out the area with Wes

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.700
to see physically if the
landscape could handle it?

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.170
- Well, normally, yeah.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.120
Like, normally we would, you know?

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.800
Normally we would go physically there

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.390
and stomp around the place

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.370
and look at it from 100 different angles

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.230
and try to figure all of that out.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.880
But we were prepping this

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.340
and having those conversations

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.430
in, like, the early mid-summer of 2020,

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.110
and it wasn't possible.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.120
So it became a totally virtual process,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.248
and we were just shooting
emails back and forth,

00:00:59.248 --> 00:01:02.280
and kind of studying, like,
all the different ways

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.620
we could go about this and, you know,

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.930
should it be more of a
location-based thing,

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:08.700
or should it be a totally built set

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.980
in a back lot but at a
studio somewhere, you know?

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.860
And so that that kind
conversation kind of was drifted

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.840
more and more and more
towards building it,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.360
but building it at a site

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.040
rather than at a studio, you know,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.450
and finding just the right place for it.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.340
- Was that something that you were excited

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.920
to land on as a choice?

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.300
- Oh yeah, yeah. That was great.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.430
It's great, you know,

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.020
and then comes all the
realities of it, right?

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.150
It got really complicated
because it's in Europe,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.770
and so it's not just
sort of one simple field

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:35.910
that you have to deal with,

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.700
but it's been subdivided
over the centuries.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.650
And so I think that,
like, the plot of land

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:45.360
where Asteroid City sat
was 137 different fields,

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.700
like, some of them, tiny, tiny tiny.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.710
And so the production locations team

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.830
and Jeremy Dawson were digging
and digging and digging

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.000
and trying to get, you know,

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.920
all these people to be on board

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.110
with this completely strange thing

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:59.514
that we were asking them to do

00:01:59.514 --> 00:02:02.176
instead of growing
watermelons that year, so...

00:02:02.176 --> 00:02:03.270
(both laugh)

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.686
- Like, building an outdoor set

00:02:04.686 --> 00:02:07.110
that has to not only be filmable

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.422
and have lights and electricity,

00:02:09.422 --> 00:02:11.610
but has to be left outside.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.340
- It had this element of,
like, civil engineering to it

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.840
that is sort of unusual,

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.910
where before we dealt with
any of the scenery going in,

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:22.298
we wired the place and we did drainage,

00:02:22.298 --> 00:02:25.695
but we laid septic lines so
that we could have bathrooms

00:02:25.695 --> 00:02:28.320
so that again, the whole
thing is functional

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.600
and the whole thing you
could just plug the lights

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.730
in to the light sockets,
and they would work.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:33.563
They would turn on.

00:02:33.563 --> 00:02:34.470
You know, you didn't have to, like,

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.240
run cables from everywhere.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.560
And it does take some time to do it,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:40.170
but it's really enjoyable to do it too,

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.840
because one of the things
about doing it this way

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.100
that we're talking about with Wes,

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.200
is it's kind of not the normal way, right?

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.917
It's not the sort of normal,

00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:51.120
"Okay, I'll be in the trailer,"
kind of way of doing it.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.580
And so a lot of it does involve

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:54.973
being the communicator of that,

00:02:54.973 --> 00:02:57.204
and being like, "No, no
we're gonna do this thing

00:02:57.204 --> 00:02:59.790
a totally different way,
and it's super exciting.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.530
And get on board, let's go. Let's do it."

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:02.363
- [Jason] Yeah, right.
- You know?

00:03:02.363 --> 00:03:04.050
But it always does seem
like there's a curve

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.550
through the process of making the film

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.620
where at the beginning
there's a lot of, like,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.027
all right I know it seems crazy, but-

00:03:09.027 --> 00:03:09.860
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.160
- You know, we're gonna do it this way.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.020
Trust me. You know?

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.130
- I'm curious to know
what it looks like now.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:17.963
- Oh, I have photos.

00:03:17.963 --> 00:03:19.920
It looks exactly like
before we arrived. Yeah.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:20.940
- That's amazing.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.530
- And because it was a
part of the agreement

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.690
with the farmers to respect their work

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.227
and what they were doing to say,

00:03:26.227 --> 00:03:28.560
"No, it's really important
to us to get this back to you

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.320
in exactly the condition
which you gave it to us."

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:32.640
So, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.786
- How is it different from imagining it,

00:03:35.786 --> 00:03:40.786
building it, to getting
it in front of the camera

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.920
making all of it real,
movable, functional,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.900
to really begin that process?

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.290
- One thing that was really
interesting on this one

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.350
was because the place is gigantic,

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.330
but it's not as gigantic as a real valley

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.340
in the, you know, in the desert, right?

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.680
It's still using all the
forced perspective tricks

00:03:58.680 --> 00:03:59.790
and techniques that you would

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.950
if you were on a sound stage.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:03.900
Just on a bigger scale, right?

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.930
And for me, I mean,
maybe because, you know,

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.075
it's the not the 1930s and
we don't do this every day.

00:04:09.075 --> 00:04:12.030
Those tricks and
techniques are a little bit

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.041
like trying to re-figure out this older,

00:04:14.041 --> 00:04:15.960
interesting way of doing things.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.231
And it comes with this, like,
"I hope I got it right."

00:04:19.231 --> 00:04:22.110
The challenging part was,
like, where should it be?

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:23.280
Should it, you know, can it be here?

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:24.870
Does it have to be here?
Does it have to be here?

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:28.260
And also, if something
isn't four miles away

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.990
sitting on the horizon
line, it's actually closer.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.220
Where's that, like, magical point

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.420
where your brain isn't gonna believe

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.010
that it's really far away?

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:37.440
And we definitely hit some snags

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.806
where a couple of our things, like,

00:04:38.806 --> 00:04:40.267
we took pictures and it was like,

00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:41.430
"It doesn't look far enough away."

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.890
And so we had to make
an artificial horizon.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:44.723
- Oh, right, right.

00:04:44.723 --> 00:04:45.690
- And so we made this kinda, like,

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.445
I don't know if you remember
from driving around,

00:04:47.445 --> 00:04:48.900
there was that little thing.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.248
And we made a absolutely
gigantic kind of perimeter-

00:04:52.248 --> 00:04:54.226
- Crater.
- Crater.

00:04:54.226 --> 00:04:56.158
That the whole set is sitting inside of.

00:04:56.158 --> 00:04:58.020
But it gave us a new horizon,

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.390
and it made all of our things work.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:02.430
And that was totally trial
and error. I had no idea.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:06.443
But when push came to shove,
it became do everything.

00:05:06.443 --> 00:05:08.460
I mean, I think a lot of
it comes from theater,

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.440
and I think a lot of it, for me,

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.900
comes from, you know,
and I have a background

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.580
in making sets on stage.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.410
And so that it's kind of second nature,

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:17.670
that part of it to me.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:18.900
But then there's this other part of it

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:22.035
where Wes and I are grabbing
frames from old films,

00:05:22.035 --> 00:05:23.580
from "The Wizard of Oz,"

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:25.147
and I'm drawing red
lines on it and saying,

00:05:25.147 --> 00:05:26.640
"I think, you know,
this is the foreground.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:27.780
This is a middle ground piece

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:29.610
that's partly in miniature.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:30.630
And then here's the background

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.340
that I think is really in miniature."

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.020
You know, and we're sending
things back and forth like that

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:37.260
trying to kind of, like,
forensically figure it out.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:38.760
And with Wes, there are layers

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:42.000
of how he got the idea for the thing,

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.110
and it's very specific.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.740
- But I mean, after working
with him now, repeatedly,

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:48.432
do you know to ask those questions

00:05:48.432 --> 00:05:51.360
or you know that's part of the...

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.060
Like, there is that forensic archeology

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.620
that's happening with...

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:56.730
You know to do that with him.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:58.500
That's-
- Yeah, for sure. For sure.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.660
And the forensic archeology
is kind of a great way

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.151
to describe the entire process,

00:06:02.151 --> 00:06:04.020
because, like, for this one,

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.460
it isn't kind of an open-ended sort of,

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:06.360
well, what is Asteroid City?

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.340
I mean, we're talking about
the big picture question,

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.920
of course, of, like, it's not
really a place on the map.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.130
It's a set of a town meant to be that way,

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:15.630
meant to feel that way.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:17.407
But then from the very beginning,

00:06:17.407 --> 00:06:19.560
he's already having shot ideas.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:20.670
He's already story boarding.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:24.240
He's already working out his
animatic version of the film.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.059
And so it is kind of a forensic process.

00:06:26.059 --> 00:06:28.680
This one was an especially
complicated puzzle,

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.870
because the sets weren't
independent things

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.900
that we could go to and then leave behind.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.780
The sets all kind of work
together, like a giant puzzle.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:38.220
- Well, that's the other
thing too, is, like,

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.938
when Wes asks you a question,

00:06:40.938 --> 00:06:43.227
"Adam, can we try this? Can we do that?"

00:06:43.227 --> 00:06:44.850
And I would watch you
come in, and you'd look,

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.274
and you guys would have
a discussion about it.

00:06:47.274 --> 00:06:49.245
How are you economic with your energies

00:06:49.245 --> 00:06:52.140
giving the person what's
needed in the moment

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.240
versus what's needed in the long term?

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:56.253
- Right, I mean, that's, that's-

00:06:56.253 --> 00:06:57.780
- That's what we're talking about.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:06:59.447
- That's the every day,
all day. That's it.

00:06:59.447 --> 00:07:01.330
- That's the trade secret.
- That's it.

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.180
- Don't say it.
- Yeah.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.250
Well, it's a total balancing act every day

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:07.440
to try to manage exactly that.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.810
Like, how can you do
the most that you can do

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:11.730
to service the last minute request

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:13.126
or the problem of right now,

00:07:13.126 --> 00:07:17.310
without hurting or endangering
the next thing, right?

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.317
And so you just do your
best to balance it.

00:07:19.317 --> 00:07:22.170
And I do my best to, like,
try to just be super clear

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:23.742
with Wes and say, "This is where I'm at."

00:07:23.742 --> 00:07:25.650
Can I ask you one?
- Sure.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:26.850
- I just...

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:28.410
Because we were talking about the sort

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:30.360
of technical forensics
nature of figuring out

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:31.350
these complicated shots,

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.810
but they do become very complex.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:35.900
And how is it for you when,

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:40.579
I assume having, trying to
have your process in quiet.

00:07:40.579 --> 00:07:44.160
Meanwhile there's this incredibly complex

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:46.530
technical set of requirements
and marks to hit.

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:49.140
And then I'm doing stuff all around you

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:51.198
with moving things and adjusting things,

00:07:51.198 --> 00:07:54.095
and I'm always fascinated watching you

00:07:54.095 --> 00:07:57.030
and kind of feeling bad, frankly,

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:59.010
that I have got so much of a circus

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:00.840
going on around you, you know?

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:01.740
- We need the circus.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:02.850
- How is that for you?

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:06.270
- Well, it's different in
every situation, I think.

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:07.800
But like, for me, that's what I love

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:11.040
about what we get to do.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:14.090
I like everyone helping each other

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:17.657
to do something for someone else.

00:08:17.657 --> 00:08:19.290
It's my dream.

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.870
It's why I, like, it's why I like music.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:23.400
I think it's 'cause it is being...

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.740
I like a band. I mean, I like a group.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.950
And it's, like, to me it's essential.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:33.030
I love that. We're all part of the take.

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:35.854
There's a scene in the movie
where I have a lighter,

00:08:35.854 --> 00:08:39.360
and I, right in the
beginning and I turn around,

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.150
and it's filled up with gasoline.

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:44.370
And I turn around, I light it.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:48.240
There's very many different
things have to occur.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:50.240
And to do it, it was...

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:55.200
The gasoline had to be the
right amount of pressure

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.390
of liquid coming out.

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:00.300
The spout had to be in the right spot.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:03.180
The lighter, if it's too wet, can't light.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:04.170
- [Adam] It's not gonna go, yeah.

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:06.330
- So many things have to go right.

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:10.260
I remember when we got a take

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:13.350
that we were like, "That's the feeling."

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:16.560
Everyone was hugging and
high fiving and stuff.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.090
And I just think that's, like,

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:20.460
that's the ultimate feeling,

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:23.817
'cause it's not, like,
it's not about one person.

00:09:23.817 --> 00:09:25.230
You know what I mean?

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:26.130
It's like-
- Totally, yeah, yeah.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:30.300
- Everybody, I love that we're
all there for each other.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:32.370
I thrive on that and I love it.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:34.560
And also, just I've never gotten to be

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:35.910
around something like this.

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:38.940
For me it was, like, this
crazy dream come true

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:41.670
that I never knew I was having.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:42.930
Like, you know what I mean?

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.900
Like, to walk down a road,
like, a forced perspective road

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.100
you're not supposed to really be able

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.189
to walk down it, I guess.

00:09:48.189 --> 00:09:49.022
- Right, right.
- 'Cause it's meant to just-

00:09:49.022 --> 00:09:50.400
- But it was big enough in full scale.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:51.750
- But it was real. Exactly.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:52.890
Like, it's bizarre.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.570
Like, it looked painted,

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:56.110
but you could drive
down it in a golf cart.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:57.434
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:57.434 --> 00:09:58.680
- And it would get...

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.050
You're very rarely in
the forced perspective.

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:03.990
And I remember one of
the last days, Wes and I,

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:07.500
we had this really wonderful day

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.660
where we kind of went and
said goodbye to the place.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:13.200
And we walked down to the end of the road.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.680
Wes put his arm up on one of
the, like, telephone poles.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:17.747
I was kind of looking around

00:10:17.747 --> 00:10:20.820
and I was like, "That's amazing."

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:24.060
I don't have much forced
perspective in my life,

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:25.606
my daily life.
- [Adam] Your daily life.

00:10:25.606 --> 00:10:27.180
- (laughs) Yeah. So I wish I did.

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:29.191
But so for me, it was, like,

00:10:29.191 --> 00:10:33.165
kind of this, like, I
couldn't get enough of it.

00:10:33.165 --> 00:10:34.644
Well, I'm just thinking about the scenes

00:10:34.644 --> 00:10:37.292
that Scarlett and I did together.

00:10:37.292 --> 00:10:39.510
You know, some of the, like,

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:40.921
I remember just one day,

00:10:40.921 --> 00:10:43.050
because of the nature of the space

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:44.700
that was, like, a little curtain,

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:47.070
like, a little instead of
a wall in this one spot,

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:48.330
that was the only kind of way

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:50.040
that I could get into this thing.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:53.550
That gives the scene
a whole other dynamic.

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:55.560
Like, the way things are constructed

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.120
helped the actors so much.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:58.273
That's what I love.

00:10:58.273 --> 00:11:00.450
I just love how the space

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:02.820
informs every decision that's made.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:04.590
- I love that that's exciting.

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:05.423
- But I know. It's the best.

00:11:05.423 --> 00:11:06.570
- Oh, oh, it's amazing.

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.418
'Cause I honestly, I'm always like,

00:11:08.418 --> 00:11:11.640
I'm sorry to tell you, you're
gonna have to turn sideways,

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:13.290
and then this, and then
there's a snake pit

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:14.123
you have to jump over.

00:11:14.123 --> 00:11:14.956
And then I feel, like,

00:11:14.956 --> 00:11:17.080
I'm always throwing these terrible

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.530
difficulties in front of you.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.960
- The coolest thing is this
movie has got so many actors

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:25.710
with so much experience
and such incredible people.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:29.370
All of them, basically the
way they looked at the alien

00:11:29.370 --> 00:11:32.040
was the way they all
looked at Asteroid City

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:33.480
for the first time when they got there.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:36.270
It's incredible, the sense of wonder

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:38.610
when something's really
beautifully crafted

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:41.040
and cared for and so special.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:42.616
Like, you just don't experience it.

00:11:42.616 --> 00:11:45.810
It doesn't matter who you are.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:48.090
It's the look in the face is the same.

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:49.740
I mean, I saw every,

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:50.940
'cause I was there from
the beginning to the end.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:52.147
I saw all these actors go.

00:11:52.147 --> 00:11:54.300
"Wesley, let's take 'em over there."

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:56.670
And it's just a testament
to you that Tom Hanks

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:59.028
has been on a lot of sets, you know,

00:11:59.028 --> 00:12:00.517
and, like, he's just like,

00:12:00.517 --> 00:12:03.150
"This is the most incredible
place I've ever been."

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:04.567
- I mean, that's so, so very kind.

00:12:04.567 --> 00:12:06.059
- Truly, just, like, the wonder

00:12:06.059 --> 00:12:10.320
and the joy and every person,
like, really, it's so unusual.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:11.280
- There was something about it.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:12.690
There was something about that place

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:14.010
that was really extraordinary.

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:15.510
And I've been really
trying to think of, like,

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:17.220
what it was.

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:19.650
And because, in a way, it was very simple.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:20.483
- Yeah.

00:12:20.483 --> 00:12:23.280
- You know, in a way,
there was a gas station

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:24.840
and there was a little lunch net

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:26.550
and there was a small motel.

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:28.590
But I kind of feel like
that sense of wonder

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:32.520
that you get of Wes' vision of the world

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:33.871
when you see the finished film,

00:12:33.871 --> 00:12:35.855
we all got to experience that

00:12:35.855 --> 00:12:39.360
in a very complete way of his vision

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:43.170
of what Asteroid City
was to him was expressed.

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:44.700
You know, we were able
to build the whole thing.

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:46.200
- Exactly.
- At one time, you know?

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:47.550
We were able to sort of lay it out,

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:48.630
and see the whole thing.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:51.559
And so in a funny way, I
think we got to experience

00:12:51.559 --> 00:12:54.030
what the audience gets to experience

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:55.890
at the end of the process.

00:12:55.890 --> 00:12:57.900
We got to live it while we were there,

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.720
which was super, super, super fun.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.780
- Well, Adam, thank you for your time.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:04.980
We should...

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:07.410
I don't want to give away
too many more trade secrets.

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:10.320
Thank you for sharing your
information and curiosity.

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:12.090
- Thank you so much.
This was really great.

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:13.285
- It's the best, the best.

00:13:13.285 --> 00:13:16.452
(bright gentle music)

